# LINUX COMMANDS CHEAT SHEET

## Navigation
- `pwd` – Prints the current working directory.
- `cd` – Changes the current directory (without arguments, goes home).
- `cd /` – Goes to the root directory.
- `cd ..` – Moves one directory up.

## Files and Directories
- `ls` – Lists files and directories.
- `ls -a` – Lists all files, including hidden files.
- `ls -la` – Lists all files with details, including hidden files.
- `mkdir` – Creates a new directory.
- `touch` – Creates an empty file.
- `cat` – Displays file contents.
- `cp` – Copies files or directories.
- `rm` – Deletes files.
- `whoami` – Displays the current username.

## Text Editors (Vi / Vim)
- `vi` – Opens a file in the Vi text editor.
- `vim` – Opens a file in the Vim text editor.
- `i` – Enters Insert Mode.
- `Esc` – Returns to Normal Mode.
- `:w` – Saves changes.
- `:q` – Exits the editor.
- `:wq` – Saves and exits.
- `:q!` – Exits without saving.

## File Permissions (chmod)
- `chmod` – Changes file or directory permissions.
- `r` – Read permission (4 / 100).
- `w` – Write permission (2 / 010).
- `x` – Execute permission (1 / 001).

### Common chmod Commands
- `chmod 755 file` – Owner: rwx, Group: r-x, Others: r-x.
- `chmod 644 file` – Owner: rw-, Group: r--, Others: r--.
- `chmod 700 file` – Owner: rwx, Group: ---, Others: ---.
- `chmod 777 file` – Everyone gets rwx.
- `chmod +x file` – Adds execute permission.
- `chmod -w file` – Removes write permission.
- `ls -l` – Checks file permissions.

